<BaseLayout
    title="Canada is the future. | Canada2080"
    description="The record of the Canada2080 kickoff on 4 October 2026 at the Markham Ballroom, with photographs in the order they were taken."
>
    <article class="recap">
        <figure class="recap-hero">
            <div class="recap-hero-media">
                <img
                    src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5347.jpg"
                    alt="Speakers and guests hold the signed Canada2080 kickoff banner in the Markham Ballroom."
                    width="2560"
                    height="1707"
                />
                <div class="recap-hero-copy">
                    <p class="eyebrow">Sunday, 4 October 2026 · Markham Ballroom</p>
                    <h1>Canada is<br />the future.</h1>
                    <p class="hero-tagline">
                        Actualizing an abundant, resilient, sustainable, and
                        sovereign future for Canada.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption class="shell">
                Speakers and guests with the kickoff banner. Hilton
                Toronto/Markham Suites Conference Centre, 4 October 2026.
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section class="highlights shell" aria-label="Event highlights">
            <p class="eyebrow">Event highlights</p>
            <ul class="highlight-grid">
                <li>
                    <p class="highlight-line">That future is not guaranteed.</p>
                    <p class="highlight-by">
                        Alan Ho. Abundance has to be shared, and resilience
                        has to be built on purpose.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p class="highlight-line">
                        An area the size of one Nepal, every year.
                    </p>
                    <p class="highlight-by">
                        Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya, on wildfire in Alberta's
                        boreal forest. 16.5 million hectares and $9.9 billion
                        a year.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p class="highlight-line">
                        Canada is the future of the planet.
                    </p>
                    <p class="highlight-by">
                        Francis Wang. In a climate-changed 2080, 3.8 billion
                        people would live outside a historical climate niche,
                        and Canada would host more of the world's people.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p class="highlight-line">
                        Know where the data sits, and who answers.
                    </p>
                    <p class="highlight-by">
                        Sunita Kumari. Sovereign AI means knowing where the
                        data sits, who can see it, and who answers for the
                        outcome.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p class="highlight-line">Start Monday.</p>
                    <p class="highlight-by">
                        Greg Hart. 2080 is not a problem to be solved. Set
                        the direction, then do the daily work.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p class="highlight-line">
                        Stop settling for a good second.
                    </p>
                    <p class="highlight-by">
                        Sebastien Gendron, on the habit of following the
                        United States or China.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="feature-band shell" aria-label="Signing the initiative">
            <p class="eyebrow">Signing the initiative</p>
            <div class="feature-grid">
                <figure>
                    <img
                        src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5329.jpg"
                        alt="Alan Ho leans over the kickoff banner and signs it."
                    />
                    <figcaption>
                        Alan Ho, York Regional Councillor for the City of
                        Markham and Chair of the Culture and Economic
                        Development Committee, signs the kickoff banner.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure>
                    <img
                        src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5332.jpg"
                        alt="Greg Hart leans over the kickoff banner and signs it."
                    />
                    <figcaption>
                        Greg Hart, co-founder and facilitator at InceptionU
                        and a member of the TransPod Advisory Board, signs
                        the kickoff banner.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure>
                    <img
                        src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5331.jpg"
                        class="is-signatures"
                        alt="Close view of handwritten signatures on the Canada2080 kickoff banner."
                    />
                    <figcaption>
                        Innovators, speakers, and guests all over Canada
                        joining and supporting the initiative.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section shell">
            <ol class="timeline">
                <li>
                    <div class="beat-copy">
                        <p class="beat-time">12:30 p.m.</p>
                        <h2>Before the first word.</h2>
                        <p>Guests were checking in, in person and online.</p>
                        <p>
                            Francis Wang hosted. Kritika Saihgpaul was master
                            of ceremonies. The programme was set for 1:00 p.m.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="beat-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <div class="beat-photos">
                        <figure>
                            <img
                                src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5340.jpg"
                                alt="Guests seated in the Markham Ballroom before the programme."
                            />
                            <figcaption>
                                Guests in the Markham Ballroom before the
                                programme. Hilton Toronto/Markham Suites
                                Conference Centre.
                            </figcaption>
                        </figure>
                    </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <div class="beat-copy">
                        <p class="beat-time">1:05 p.m.</p>
                        <h2>The future is not guaranteed.</h2>
                        <h3>
                            Alan Ho
                            <StatementLink
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-alan-ho"
                                label="Alan Ho's statement"
                            />
                        </h3>
                        <p class="speaker-role">
                            York Regional Councillor for the City of Markham.
                            Chair of the Culture and Economic Development
                            Committee.
                        </p>
                        <p class="speaker-link">
                            <a
                                href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/alan-ho-976aa03b/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
                            >
                            <a href="/events/2026-kickoff-alan-ho#full-transcript">Full Transcript</a>
                        </p>
                        <p>He opened the afternoon.</p>
                        <p>
                            The welcome was about 2080: a future of abundance
                            and resilience. Soaring technology, cleaner energy,
                            longer lives, and thriving communities are a
                            picture, he said. They are not a promise.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Abundance has to be shared. Resilience has to be
                            built on purpose. Both depend on choices made now.
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Protect water, parks, and climate.</li>
                            <li>Plan housing and transit for every generation.</li>
                            <li>
                                Take up innovation, and still safeguard dignity.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Strengthen local food, health, and social
                                connection.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>
                            Resilience, in his definition, is more than
                            surviving a shock. It is adapting, recovering, and
                            growing together. Abundance is more than having
                            more. It is enough opportunity, and enough hope,
                            for everyone.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            He pictured Markham, and places beyond it, where
                            seniors are supported, young people can afford to
                            stay, newcomers belong, and neighbourhoods stay
                            green and safe. That, he said, takes courage,
                            collaboration, and a longer view than the next
                            political cycle.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="beat-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <div class="beat-photos">
                        <figure>
                            <img
                                src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5349.jpg"
                                alt="Alan Ho speaking at the podium during the civic welcome."
                            />
                            <figcaption>
                                Alan Ho, York Regional Councillor for the City
                                of Markham, opening the civic welcome.
                            </figcaption>
                        </figure>
                        <figure>
                            <img
                                src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5350.jpg"
                                alt="Francis Wang and Alan Ho hold a framed City of Markham proclamation for the Canada2080 kickoff."
                            />
                            <figcaption>
                                Francis Wang and Alan Ho with the City of
                                Markham proclamation for the kickoff.
                            </figcaption>
                        </figure>
                    </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <div class="beat-copy">
                        <p class="beat-time">1:10 p.m.</p>
                        <h2>Canada is the future of the planet.</h2>
                        <h3>
                            Francis Wang
                            <StatementLink
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-francis-wang"
                                label="Francis Wang's statement"
                            />
                        </h3>
                        <p class="speaker-role">
                            Opening keynote, Canada2080: the long-horizon
                            challenge.
                        </p>
                        <p class="speaker-link">
                            <a
                                href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/findcongwang/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
                            >
                            <a href="/events/2026-kickoff-francis-wang#full-transcript"
                                >Full Transcript</a
                            >
                        </p>
                        <p>He called it a kickoff, not a multi-day launch.</p>
                        <p>
                            Canada2080, in his words, is a long-horizon
                            initiative. Gather people who believe in similar
                            possibilities, start the conversations, and hold
                            many more.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The claim he set out: for Canada to be one of the
                            most prosperous nations by 2080, and to say that
                            Canada is the future of planet Earth.
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                Foundations already in hand: land, freshwater,
                                rare earths, biomaterials, clean power, research
                                institutions, and zero-to-one opportunities.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                The failure, as he put it: firms struggle to
                                commercialize and scale, intellectual property
                                goes to foreign corporations, educated people
                                leave, and raw materials are sold instead of
                                becoming local supply chains. He called that a
                                trade of intellectual culture and resource value
                                for cheap money, and a plantation for the world.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                American industrialists built pipelines so the industry stayed in America.
                                The Bay Area, he said, is where entrepreneurs
                                make the money, then sometimes fund training
                                institutions whose talent is funnelled
                                elsewhere.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                The ambition: resilient living systems,
                                manufacturing, an integrated supply chain, and
                                domestic chips. By then, he said, they would be
                                quantum chips. He said it took Taiwan 40 years
                                to lead in chip manufacturing.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Looking back from 2080, 3.8 billion people would
                                be living outside a historical climate niche and
                                would need to go somewhere. He pointed to an
                                equal-earth map and said the Southern Hemisphere
                                has little land, so the movement would be north,
                                leaving Canada and Russia to host more of the
                                world's people. A larger population would
                                support the density patterns he associates with
                                Asia, and the workforce for a domestic supply
                                chain.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Four retentions, and Canada needs all four.
                                Capability keeps the means to build and operate.
                                Ownership and decision rights keep the choice of
                                direction. Learning and reinvestment keep the
                                next success connected to the one after it.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                AI and infrastructure, he said, are tightly
                                coupled. Scale requires energy. From this point,
                                he argued, scale also requires compute. He
                                quoted forward guidance from the past month:
                                "Controlling our energy means controlling our
                                destiny."
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Neighbourhoods that generate their own energy,
                                compute, food, and water. Household compute for
                                health, finances, and private records, with
                                spare capacity returned to the connected system.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                He took the room from dial-up to cable modems,
                                as a computer science graduate from Waterloo. He
                                thanked people who had bought gaming computers,
                                and called that an investment in Canada's
                                sovereign compute.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="beat-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <div class="beat-photos">
                        <figure>
                            <img
                                src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5351.jpg"
                                alt="Francis Wang speaking at the podium. The slide behind him reads bold ambition by 2080."
                            />
                            <figcaption>
                                Francis Wang. The slide behind him is the
                                long-horizon challenge.
                            </figcaption>
                        </figure>
                    </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <div class="beat-copy">
                        <p class="beat-time">1:20 p.m.</p>
                        <h2>Sovereign AI, close to the problem.</h2>
                        <p>
                            Kritika Saihgpaul introduced the set. Five speakers
                            followed. The banking topic in one introduction
                            belonged to Sunita Kumari, not to the wildfire talk.
                        </p>
                        <h3>
                            Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya
                            <StatementLink
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-sudarshana-bhattacharya"
                                label="Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya's statement"
                            />
                        </h3>
                        <p class="speaker-link">
                            <a
                                href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sudarshanabhattacharya/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
                            >
                            <a
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-sudarshana-bhattacharya#full-transcript"
                                >Full Transcript</a
                            >
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            She had just graduated from Golden Gate University.
                            At work she is a senior director analyst. She kept
                            the remarks on what Canada already has, and on one
                            urgent problem that returns every year: wildfire in
                            Alberta's boreal forest.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Her geographic context: every summer, wildfire burns
                            an area the size of one Nepal. She put the numbers
                            at 16.5 million hectares and $9.9 billion at stake
                            each year. Indigenous communities who live there are most affected. The
                            timber industry is hit hardest.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            AI, as she used the word, is not only ChatGPT. She
                            dated the field at 70 years, and said that older
                            practice does not need the compute that GPT models
                            or Gemini need. That gap is where her research sits.
                        </p>
                        <p>The thesis architecture has two layers.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                A foundation pulls the data together and cleans
                                it. She said that step does not need the compute
                                people assume.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                An intelligence layer starts with Random Forest,
                                which she said can run on an ordinary computer,
                                then a convolutional network that tries to see
                                the way an eye does, then an ANN that brings
                                the parts together.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>
                            The aim is a calculated dollar figure for forest-fire
                            exposure. She would implement it with small,
                            domain-specific language models, using Natural
                            Resources Canada and Statistics Canada. Geospatial
                            data is the hard remaining set. She has a project
                            charter for a master's capstone. Some Copernicus
                            satellite data from Europe may still be needed. The
                            point, she said, is a usable picture of what is at
                            stake, built as much as possible on data held here.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="beat-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <div class="beat-photos">
                        <figure>
                            <img
                                src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5363.jpg"
                                alt="Dr Sudarshana Bhattacharya speaking at the podium in a white blazer."
                            />
                            <figcaption>
                                On wildfire exposure in Alberta's boreal forest.
                            </figcaption>
                        </figure>
                    </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <div class="beat-copy">
                        <h3>
                            Dr Kimberly Yazzie
                            <StatementLink
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-kimberly-yazzie"
                                label="Dr Kimberly Yazzie's statement"
                            />
                        </h3>
                        <p class="speaker-link">
                            <a
                                href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/kimberly-yazzie-ph-d-09758a4a/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
                            >
                            <a
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-kimberly-yazzie#full-transcript"
                                >Full Transcript</a
                            >
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Joining remotely, she spoke in her own capacity, not
                            on behalf of her institution. Sovereign AI, she
                            said, is usually who owns the compute, the data, and
                            the models. She asked a different question: for
                            whom?
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            For Indigenous communities, she said, AI is a
                            question of relationships. Who decides, who
                            benefits, and who carries the responsibility after a
                            project ends. Satellites and sensors can be a tool
                            for care. Built without the people of that land,
                            they can become a way of watching them. It is not
                            her place to speak for any community. Indigenous
                            data sovereignty and early engagement, she said,
                            deserve real support.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Every community can take up a new technology, or
                            not, on its own terms. When it steps in, education
                            and partnership start with the first conversation.
                        </p>
                        <p>Two commitments make that choice meaningful.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                Consent that stays open. Free, prior, and
                                informed consent is an ongoing relationship, not
                                a checkbox. A community can revisit its choice,
                                and data stays under the authority of the
                                community it describes.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Accountability to relationships, not just
                                outputs. AI has to move with Indigenous
                                governance, not in a direction set only from
                                the outside.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>
                            She cited the UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics
                            of Artificial Intelligence: Indigenous peoples must
                            take part in shaping AI, and the task is to make
                            room for that in practice. Her proposal is to begin
                            with the partnership, not a product. Her link
                            dropped before the panel question.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="beat-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <div class="beat-photos">
                        <figure>
                            <img
                                src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5373.jpg"
                                alt="Dr Kimberly Yazzie on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely."
                            />
                            <figcaption>
                                Dr Kimberly Yazzie, on the screen.
                            </figcaption>
                        </figure>
                    </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <div class="beat-copy">
                        <h3>
                            Dr Erin Trochim
                            <StatementLink
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-erin-trochim"
                                label="Dr Erin Trochim's statement"
                            />
                        </h3>
                        <p class="speaker-link">
                            <a
                                href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/erin-trochim/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
                            >
                            <a
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-erin-trochim#full-transcript"
                                >Full Transcript</a
                            >
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Also remote. She joined as a Canadian living in
                            Alaska. A vast amount of geospatial data exists.
                            Very little of it reaches the person who needs it
                            when something breaks.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Her story was a door stuck closed, after an
                            earthquake, a house on permafrost, or a single frost
                            heave. The record has often known about the movement
                            for a long time. NASA's NISAR products track it. The
                            question is whether somebody tells the homeowner,
                            and what happens next. Too often it goes straight to
                            the insurer.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The data is plentiful and, on the jurisdictional
                            side, still not connected. New systems need common
                            protocols across individuals, municipalities, tribal
                            nations, and state and federal governments, with
                            authority retained by the participants. Cognitive
                            offloading hands thought to the model. She wants the
                            other half kept: the ability to question a finding,
                            and a choice about who the system does more for.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            For Canada in 2080 she offered four tests of
                            control, drawn from community planning she wants
                            embedded in the information system.
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                Contestability: the people affected can contest
                                or refuse a consequential decision.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Dependency: participants can operate, interpret,
                                maintain, and negotiate without one vendor or
                                one expert.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Value intention: community assets are
                                strengthened or extracted, and the difference
                                has to be visible.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Adaptability: institutions and communities can
                                exit, provide, substitute, or repair.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="beat-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <div class="beat-photos">
                        <figure>
                            <img
                                src="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5380.jpg"
                                alt="Dr Erin Trochim on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely."
                            />
                            <figcaption>
                                Dr Erin Trochim, on the screen.
                            </figcaption>
                        </figure>
                    </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <div class="beat-copy">
                        <h3>
                            Sunita Kumari
                            <StatementLink
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-sunita-kumari"
                                label="Sunita Kumari's statement"
                            />
                        </h3>
                        <p class="speaker-link">
                            <a
                                href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sunita-kumari-data-ai/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
                            >
                            <a
                                href="/events/2026-kickoff-sunita-kumari#full-transcript"
                                >Full Transcript</a
                            >
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            AI is writing code and taking on human tasks. The
                            other side, she said, is risk: hallucinations,
                            biased responses, and exposed customer data. That
                            is where governance starts.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Governance enforces the controls that keep a model
                            operating responsibly. Sovereign AI is who controls
                            the environment: where the data resides, where the
                            infrastructure is, who can see the data, and who
                            answers for the outcome.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Her example was a bank's mortgage agent that keeps
                            denying applicants with a strong credit history. If
                            the training data carries a location preference, the
                            responses will be biased too. Governance makes the
                            output transparent, sends a suspicious result to a
                            person, and that person can ask for a retrain.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            People think governance slows innovation. She said
                            it is how you scale with confidence. She compared
                            the early fear of putting a credit card online,
                            which receded once anti-fraud controls existed.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="beat-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <div class="beat-photos">
